e by  government  make citizens insecure I mean that's why  we don't all move to China or didn't all  move to East Germany or former Iraq that  government surveillance is toxic for  governments to do and is toxic for  populations to be subjected to which is  why when we built the United States  Constitution we put in very strong rules  about police surveillance and now it had  to be tailored and targeted and minimal  and necessary these programs fly in the  face of that and that makes us less  secure we're not saying that the FBI the  NSA shouldn't conduct surveillance it's  that broad surveillance of populations  is bad for our security and on top of  that it doesn't actually give us  security against criminals and  terrorists and all those other things  that sort of another half of it this is  really the way to look at it and  unfortunately this is hard because when  people are scared they willing to say do  whatever it takes to make me feel less  scared even though it might be  ineffective you know it might be  corrosive people going to go along with  it I'm going to get into the details  later in the program about some of the  past finds that Yahoo was was a victim  of with regard to not participating in  prism but can you talk to us a little  generally from your knowledge what are  some of the ways or maybe a better way  to say it is how prevalent is it that  government agencies will try to  strong-arm some of these big tech  companies that have a lot of data into  playing ball with them either in terms  of data turnover or back doors etc how  does that function in the real world you  know we don't know a lot of details we  just see snippets of it so we know in  one case the NSA bribed RSA to put it  back door one of their encryption  systems so we know that we know that a  lot comes from legal compulsion we saw  the verizon order that was one of the  Snowden documents now we're seeing this  Yahoo demand we see bits from different  companies transparency reports my guess  is that when you had them are  traditional companies the telcos there  was a tradition  playing along so we know that the NSA  put Massa be dropping equipment in AT&amp;T  s data centers around the country and  they did that by asking nicely but 18t  had a history of cooperating with the  NSA throughout the Cold War so this was  an easy thing to do when they did the  same thing to becoming like Google will  probably said go away we're not going to  do that which is why they had to resort  to other mechanisms so I think there's a  lot of culture between the telco culture  and the Silicon Valley culture and the  differences and it's a lot about  relationships now you want to help if  you're approached by the US government  and then told there's an attack imminent  please help keep America safe it's hard  to say no but it shouldn't be hard to  say get a warrant it shouldn't be hard  to say go before a judge follow the law  and then we will help you seal up would  you feel differently about a lot of this  if instead of knowing that particularly  the bulk surveillance that was exposed  by the Snowden leaks and subsequently by  the initially incorrect statements of  the NSA regarding how effective this  type of surveillance is would you feel  differently broadly speaking about this  type of surveillance if we found out it  really was effective at preventing real  real-life violence terrorist attacks etc  the Fourth Amendment's always about  trade-offs and we'd have to look at that  and if it turned out that these tools  were extraordinarily effective we would  have to look at the cost look at the  benefits and make a trade off if you  think about it we already give the  police extraordinary power to peer into  a personalized why because we know it  solves crimes we know it keeps us safe  we just have mechanisms around that to  ensure the police don't abuse their  authority and more or less they work so  it really isn't absolute it's about  figuring out what the limits are and  figuring out how to give authorities  these capabilities without losing our  liberties without losing our freedoms  and we can do that we've done that in  the past we  do in the future but we have to have the  facts to make these decisions as a  society I'd like to talk a little bit  with you about computer security in the  context of the upcoming election and  every election there are observed broad  range of different concerns and  allegations that crop up regarding often  the tampering of voting machines Donald  Trump has been more frequently using the  term rig than whether it applies  exclusively to voting machines it  appears the answer is no but that may be  part of it up from a computer security  perspective when we think about a  national election which is really 50  individual state elections often managed  in in pretty drastically different ways  what are the concerns that everybody  should sort of generally be aware of  when it comes to computer security I  think about the election security there  are three areas that I worry about yeah  the first one is a reservation system  that a lot of havoc can be raped by  deleting or changing voter rolls people  try to vote and they can't because  they're not on the system there have  been probes in several states there have  been tests a lot of shoddy security  there the second is election machines  themselves a lot of them are vulnerable  to hacking some of them in key states  especially the ones that are touchscreen  only without paper audit trails and the  third is the tabulation and  dissemination that after we vote those  totals are sent up the chain and  aggregated and there's a lot of room for  attacks there so looking at those three  steps those are the vulnerabilities and  I think we need to really apply some  technology here it's a bit late for this  election you have to really pay  attention and use humans to try to  secure our system but I worry i worry  about Russian involvement I worry about  kids playing politics hmm i think these  offer important concerns lesson the  presidential election more in local  elections where little changes can make  a big difference but i think we start  have to worry about our election system  and whether it is critical national  infrastructure in the same way our power  grid is and i think you can make a case  that it is from a purely sort of  logistical and technical standpoint of  the three areas of concern that you  mentioned which would be the easiest for  sort of a lone wolf to affect and which  might be more on the spectrum of  requiring a coordinated effort you know  pretty much everything can be done by a  lone wolf I mean this is the thing about  computer security it doesn't require a  nation state to do it it could be two  guys in the basement somewhere so that  really isn't the the factor what really  matters is whether it will be effective  you know and if you think about it if  you wanted to swing the US presidential  election it's not easy you're not going  to know where the hinge points will be  you can't do a lot of swinging because  you know it'll be noticed more worrisome  or local elections state elections city  elections county elections where there  are much fewer eyes on it where the  stakes are smaller where there's more  petty infighting I worry they're more if  you think what's happening about this  election we're seeing Russia do kind of  a disinformation campaign which really  isn't attacking the election but  attacking the information process  leading up to the election and that's  something that a nation-state might do  you know we talk a lot about election  vulnerabilities and they're serious  pulling it off as stole a lot of luck  and a lot of skill and I think that's in  our favor but we really should secure  our systems because we don't have a  process if there's a problem I mean we  don't have a system to vote over a week  later right to fix it if the we open up  the machines and the votes are old 00 we  just don't know what to do and after an  election there are sides now we can  discuss what to do and nobody cares the  day after election day half of the  country is going to say fix it other  half is going to say don't fix it it's  fine right it'll become partisan so  whatever we do we have to do now before  we know the results no doubt about that  we've been speaking with Bruce Schneier  he is a fellow at the Harvard Kennedy  School really a pleasure speaking with  you thank you so much thank you

